# Task: chebyshev_relu_d7
Degree-7 Chebyshev polynomial approximation of the relu activation on [-4, 4] (benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks).
Inputs: ciphertext `x` (values in [-4, 4]); plaintext scalars: `alpha` = 0.25 (maps x to z = x/4 in [-1, 1]), `one` = 1.0, and c0 ... c7, the Chebyshev coefficients (each one is the same in every slot).
Output: `y` = c0 + sum_k c_k T_k(z), with T0 = 1, T1 = z and T_k = 2 z T_{k-1} - T_{k-2}. The coefficients c1, c2, c4, c6 and c0 are non-negligible; the rest are ~1e-16 and can be dropped.
Any mathematically equivalent evaluation is allowed (another basis or evaluation order), as long as it only uses these inputs.

Correctness: every output must be within 1e-06 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; Chebyshev relu degree 7 on [-4, 4], as in benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  vector<Ciphertext> t(8);
  t[1] = z;
  Ciphertext z_square = z * z;
  t[2] = z_square + z_square - one;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 7; ++k)
  {
    Ciphertext z_t = z * t[k - 1];
    t[k] = z_t + z_t - t[k - 2];
  }
  Ciphertext result;
  bool has_result = false;
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Ciphertext term1 = t[1] * c1;
  result = has_result ? result + term1 : term1;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Ciphertext term2 = t[2] * c2;
  result = has_result ? result + term2 : term2;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Ciphertext term4 = t[4] * c4;
  result = has_result ? result + term4 : term4;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext term6 = t[6] * c6;
  result = has_result ? result + term6 : term6;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 152.34 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 7
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (19, 7); IR terms: 64
operations: ct_ct_mul=5, square=0, ct_pt_mul=5, relin=5, rotate=0, rescale=10, mod_switch=10, match_scale=7, add=14
max abs error vs expected: 1.07e-07

# How a kernel is judged
One timing is too noisy to rank kernels: the same circuit can measure several percent faster or slower from run to run. So a correct kernel becomes the new best only if
1. its circuit differs from the baseline's, the current best's and every earlier attempt's (circuit = CHEHAB's depth and complete operation counts), and
2. it is then confirmed faster than the best when both are timed in alternating batches (at least 3% faster, winning most batches).
Rewrites that leave the circuit unchanged (renaming, reordering, regrouping that CHEHAB normalizes away) cannot win. Aim for fewer ciphertext multiplications (including square), rotations, plaintext multiplications, rescales and scale alignments, or fewer levels.

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

Idea: fold the polynomial into an even/odd form in x and cut the multiplications.

Only c0, c1, c2, c4, c6 are kept, so the result is c0 + c1·T1 + c2·T2 + c4·T4 + c6·T6. T1 is odd. T2, T4 and T6 are even polynomials in z, so they can be written as polynomials in u = z².

- T2 = 2u − 1
- T4 = 8u² − 8u + 1
- T6 = 32u³ − 48u² + 18u − 1

So y = c0 + c1·z + P(u), where P(u) = a1·u + a2·u² + a3·u³ + a0.
The coefficients are:
- a3 = 32·c6
- a2 = 8·c4 − 48·c6
- a1 = 2·c2 − 8·c4 + 18·c6
- a0 = −c2 + c4 − c6

These coefficients are plaintext arithmetic, which is not allowed on its own because every operation needs a ciphertext operand. That cost is avoided by never forming a coefficient as a plaintext: I use the ciphertext-side structure instead. The constants 2, 8, 18, 32 and 48 can only come from additions of ciphertexts, which is expensive.

A cheaper route keeps the Chebyshev recurrence but removes the unused odd terms:
- z = x·alpha (one plaintext multiply)
- u = z² (one multiplication)
- T2 = 2u − one
- T4 = 2·T2² − one (one multiplication)
- T6 = 2·T2·T4 − T2 (one multiplication)

That is 3 ciphertext multiplications instead of 5, and depth 3 instead of 7.
The sum is c1·z + c2·T2 + c4·T4 + c6·T6 + c0, with 4 plaintext multiplies.

To save a level on z, u = z² = x²·alpha². Computing x² first and then multiplying by alpha² would need a plaintext product, which is not allowed. So I use u = z·z directly.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext m = t2 * t4;
  Ciphertext t6 = m + m - t2;
  Ciphertext result = z * c1 + t2 * c2 + t4 * c4 + t6 * c6 + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: factor the even part as a cubic in t = T2, so T4 and T6 are never formed separately.

- T4 = 2t² − 1 and T6 = t(4t² − 3).
- y = c0 + c1·z + c4·(2s − 1) + t·(c2 + c6·(4s − 3)), with s = t².
- This needs 3 ct-ct multiplications (u, s, and the final product by t) and 4 plaintext multiplications (alpha, c1, c4, c6), one fewer than before. Depth stays at 5.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t);
  Ciphertext s2 = s + s;
  Ciphertext t4 = s2 - one;
  Ciphertext h = s2 + s2 - one - one - one;
  Ciphertext inner = h * c6 + c2;
  Ciphertext result = t * inner + t4 * c4 + z * c1 + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: cut the depth to 4 by moving the c6 multiplication onto t, which is available early and runs in parallel with s. This costs one extra plaintext multiplication (c2·t) but removes a level from the critical path.

y = c0 + c1·z + c4·(2s − 1) + (c6·t)·(4s − 3) + c2·t

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t);
  Ciphertext s2 = s + s;
  Ciphertext t4 = s2 - one;
  Ciphertext h = s2 + s2 - one - one - one;
  Ciphertext tc6 = t * c6;
  Ciphertext result = tc6 * h + t4 * c4 + t * c2 + z * c1 + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The previous attempt failed because c4·T4 and (c6·t)·h met at the same level with different multiplication histories. This version removes that sum by factoring s out of the whole even part:

y = s·(4·c6·t + 2·c4) + (c2·t − 3·c6·t) + c1·z + c0 − c4

- The ct-ct product s·g is the only deep term, at level 4. g = 4·(c6·t) + c4 + c4 is formed at level 3.
- Every other term is at level 3 or lower and uses the same pt-multiplication pattern.
- Operation counts: 3 ct-ct multiplications and 4 plaintext multiplications (alpha, c6·t, c2·t, c1·z), at depth 4.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t);
  Ciphertext tc6 = t * c6;
  Ciphertext tc6_2 = tc6 + tc6;
  Ciphertext g = tc6_2 + tc6_2 + c4 + c4;
  Ciphertext low = t * c2 - (tc6_2 + tc6) + z * c1 + c0 - c4;
  Ciphertext result = s * g + low;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: start from the depth-4 kernel and compute s = T2² as 4u² − 4u + 1 from u directly. That makes u² a square of u that does not depend on t. The circuit is the same depth with a different operation mix (adds replace one dependence), and the lower-level adds may be cheaper.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext u2 = square(u);
  Ciphertext u2d = u2 + u2;
  Ciphertext ud = u + u;
  Ciphertext s = u2d + u2d - ud - ud + one;
  Ciphertext tc6 = t * c6;
  Ciphertext tc6_2 = tc6 + tc6;
  Ciphertext g = tc6_2 + tc6_2 + c4 + c4;
  Ciphertext low = t * c2 - (tc6_2 + tc6) + z * c1 + c0 - c4;
  Ciphertext result = s * g + low;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The last variant was slower, so I go back to attempt 2 and trim its additions. Since T6 = t·(2·T4 − 1), the term 4s − 3 equals 2·T4 − 1, so h = t4 + t4 − one needs 2 additions instead of 5.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t);
  Ciphertext t4 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext h = t4 + t4 - one;
  Ciphertext inner = h * c6 + c2;
  Ciphertext result = t * inner + t4 * c4 + z * c1 + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: factor T4 out of the c4 and c6 terms. T6 = t·(2·T4 − 1), so the result is

y = T4·(2·c6·t + c4) + (c2·t − c6·t) + c1·z + c0.

This keeps depth at 4 with 3 ct-ct multiplications and 4 plaintext multiplications, and needs only 4 plaintext additions (−one twice, +c4, +c0).

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t);
  Ciphertext t4 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext tc6 = t * c6;
  Ciphertext g = tc6 + tc6 + c4;
  Ciphertext low = t * c2 - tc6 + z * c1 + c0;
  Ciphertext result = t4 * g + low;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

This is the last attempt, so I make a small variation on attempt 6, the fastest kernel so far. Instead of forming h = 2·T4 − 1 as a ciphertext, I apply c6 to 2·T4 and subtract c6 afterwards as a plaintext. This swaps the order of the plaintext operations, and may let CHEHAB schedule levels and scales differently.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t);
  Ciphertext t4 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext w = t4 + t4;
  Ciphertext inner = w * c6 - c6 + c2;
  Ciphertext result = t * inner + t4 * c4 + z * c1 + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```
